  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nTonight through Labor Day Weekend...Strong high pressure aloft will \nremain in place across the region through the forecast period. This, \nas high pressure at the surface likewise remains wedged into the \nforecast area from the east.  Despite \"Potential Tropical Cyclone \nFive\" currently centered off the south central Louisiana gulf coast, \nthis system is not forecast to affect the weather across our area as \nit continues to move west northwest towards the Southwest \nLouisiana/Southeast Texas coast over the next 36 hours.\n\nTherefore for us through this forecast period, expect a continuation \nof increasingly humid conditions and hot temperatures. Moisture will \nsubtly build across the region through the end of the week. Highs \neach afternoon will warm into the middle and upper 90s. This, as dew \npoints mix each day in the low and middle 70s, will yield afternoon \nheat index values above the century mark in most locations.  Across \nportions of the area during the week, indices will hover between 105-\n110 degrees.  This will result in a \"Heat Advisory\" being needed and \nlikely adjusted across the forecast area each day as dangerous heat \nstress conditions are expected to impact portions of, if not all of, \nthe CWA through the week.\n\nOther than an isolated shower or storm being observed in the south \nin the vicinity of the Highway 84 corridor today through Wednesday, \nmuch of the CWA will remain dry.  As better moisture works north \ninto the CWA Thursday into the weekend, better rain chances will \nlikewise spread northward into the CWA.  This could bring some \ntemporary relief from both the heat and dry conditions across the \nregion, especially Friday into Labor Day Weekend. /19/",
